{-
   A producer in a module of its own, for `iter/Name.yana`'s path through an import -
   Design-Iterator-Objects.md §2.4's "across a module boundary".

   Two declarations and the difference between them is `pub`: a site type is named through the
   ordinary lookup, so what an importer may write `Lexing.digits.Iter` about is exactly what it may
   call, and `private` is what `Reject.Name.yana` names to show it.
-}

module Lexing

-- The digits of `n`, most significant last. An object of this is `digits.Iter` here and
-- `Lexing.digits.Iter` anywhere that imports it.
pub iter fn digits(n: I32) -> I32:
    let &rest = n

    while rest > 0:
        yield rest % 10
        rest = rest / 10

-- Not exported, so a path to its object type is the same "not in scope" a call of it would be.
iter fn private(n: I32) -> I32:
    yield n

-- The same object built here, so that an importer can write the result type this answers.
pub fn theDigits(n: I32) -> digits.Iter:
    let it = digits(n)
    return it
